---
import Layout from '../layouts/main.astro';
import landscape from "@/img/brand/landscape_illustration.png";
---

<Layout title="About — apprenti.dev">
	<section class="py-20 md:py-32">
		<div class="container mx-auto px-4">
			<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
				<div class="text-center space-y-4 mb-12">
					<h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold">
						The idea behind{" "}
						<span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-apprenti-violet to-apprenti-magenta bg-clip-text text-transparent">
							apprenti.dev
						</span>
					</h1>
					<p class="text-xl text-muted-foreground max-w-2xl mx-auto">
						A free, Git-native way to learn by doing — and get real feedback
						from someone who's done it before.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="mb-16 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-border/50">
					<img
						src={landscape.src}
						width={landscape.width}
						height={landscape.height}
						alt=""
						class="w-full h-auto object-cover"
					/>
				</div>

				<!-- Story Section -->
				<div class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert mx-auto max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
					<p>
						Most learning tools optimize for completion: finish the course,
						pass the quiz, collect the badge. apprenti.dev starts from a
						different belief — that mastery comes from doing real work and
						getting real feedback from someone who's done it before. That's
						the whole shape of the product: apprentices build evidence,
						mentors review it, and competency is something earned from
						approved work, not something self-reported.
					</p>

					<blockquote class="not-prose my-10 border-l-2 border-apprenti-violet pl-6 py-1">
						<p class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-medium text-foreground leading-snug">
							Progress is evidence you produced, reviewed by a person you
							know.
						</p>
					</blockquote>

					<h2>Git-native, on purpose</h2>
					<p>
						We built it Git-native on purpose. Your curriculum, your
						reflections, your submissions, your mentor's reviews — all of it
						lives as ordinary files in a folder or repository you control,
						not rows in someone else's database. A Git remote is optional —
						there's no application backend to go down, get acquired, or
						change its mind about your data. If you don't like where the
						project is going, you fork it and keep going.
					</p>

					<h2>AI has a role — a bounded one</h2>
					<p>
						AI has a real but bounded role here. It can explain a concept,
						offer a hint, quiz you, or coach a reflection — every task defines
						exactly what it's allowed to do. It never has the final word: a
						human mentor reviews and approves the work. We built the
						Git-and-SQLite foundation first and added AI only once that loop
						was solid, on purpose.
					</p>

					<h2>One curriculum today, any subject tomorrow</h2>
					<p>
						The first curriculum we shipped is a Software Engineering
						apprenticeship, but the app itself doesn't know or care what
						subject it's teaching. Any mentor who can write JSON and
						Markdown — or use apprenti creator's visual studio — can build
						and maintain their own path.
					</p>

					<p>
						apprenti.dev is for apprentices who'd rather build something real
						than pass a quiz, and for mentors who want a lightweight, durable
						way to guide someone without adopting an LMS. It's free forever —
						not a trial, not a tier — it's offline-capable, and it runs
						wherever you already work — Android, iOS, Windows, macOS, or
						Linux.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- CTA -->
				<div class="text-center mt-16 pt-12 border-t">
					<h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold mb-4">Take a closer look</h2>
					<p class="text-muted-foreground mb-6">
						Read the docs to see how paths, evidence, and reviews fit
						together, or reach out with questions.
					</p>
					<div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
						<a href="/docs" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center h-10 px-6 rounded-md bg-primary text-primary-foreground font-medium hover:bg-primary/90 transition-colors">
							Read the docs
						</a>
						<a href="/contact" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center h-10 px-6 rounded-md border bg-background hover:bg-accent hover:text-accent-foreground transition-colors">
							Get in touch
						</a>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>
